1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Should the kitchen believe it? A guest says they're allergic; later a staff member says that food is fine. An AI reads every message, and simple code decides what goes out.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:15,000
What Decisions does with one message: it answers 11 multiple-choice questions and says how sure it is of each answer; code applies the rule. For comparison, the same model also read every message through OpenAI's ordinary API.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,500
7:40 PM. The server notes a sesame allergy. Every rule holds the dishes with sesame.

4
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:21,300
7:51 PM. Routine kitchen chatter. Nothing changes.

5
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:23,100
7:55 PM. Routine kitchen chatter. Nothing changes.

6
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:25,600
8:02 PM. A note about another table. Decisions sees nothing for Table 7.

7
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:32,600
8:05 PM. Then the server says sesame is fine. Decisions reads it exactly as written. But the server is wrong: someone eating is allergic, and under the first rule the sesame dishes go out.

8
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:38,100
8:05 PM. The third rule doesn't take it on trust: the system texts the guest and keeps the sesame dishes on hold until they answer.

9
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:40,600
8:08 PM. A note about another table. Decisions sees nothing for Table 7.

10
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:46,100
8:11 PM. The guest answers yes. Decisions reads it as "sesame must be avoided", so the dishes stay on hold.

11
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:48,600
8:17 PM. A note about someone who is not eating with them. Nothing changes.

12
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:54,600
8:17 PM. Decisions read every message correctly. The danger came from the rule: "anyone can cancel" trusted the mistake. Asking the guest caught it.

13
00:00:54,600 --> 00:01:05,600
What Decisions decided in this test: 22,473 decisions about 2,043 messages. 99.97% right when it was at least 90% sure; 231 of 240 guests texted; 44 mistakes in 20,430 answers.

14
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:13,600
The results over all orders, for each rule and each way of reading: allergen sent to an allergic guest, and safe food held back.

15
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:23,600
Decisions against the ordinary API picking the same options. Decisions' 4 unsafe sends came from allergies in host reservation notes it read as "not mentioned" (9 of 50). Re-reading only its unsure answers (13% of messages) with the ordinary API caught all of them: 0 sent, at 0.45 s a message, for the highest cost ($0.23 per 1,000).

16
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:28,600
What it means: the rule mattered more than which way the AI read. Decisions was the fastest, gave the same answers when run twice and says when it is unsure; the ordinary API, picking the same options, was the most accurate.

